## Controlled Vulnerability Creation

A temporary test directory was created on WIN11-02:

```text
C:\VULN31TEST
```

A harmless test file was added:

```text
C:\VULN31TEST\test.txt
```

The folder was then exposed as an SMB share:

```text
\\WIN11-02\VULN31TEST
```

using:

```powershell
New-SmbShare -Name VULN31TEST -Path C:\VULN31TEST -FullAccess Everyone
```

This intentionally created:

```text
Everyone
Full Control
```

at the SMB share level.

---

## VULN-001 Discovery

The share was identified using:

```powershell
Get-SmbShare
```

Share permissions were reviewed using:

```powershell
Get-SmbShareAccess -Name VULN31TEST
```

The vulnerable configuration was:

```text
Share:
VULN31TEST

Principal:
Everyone

Access:
Full Control
```

This became:

```text
VULN-001 — Overly Permissive SMB Share
```

---

## Remote Validation

The finding was validated from WIN11-01.

The remote share was enumerated using:

```cmd
dir \\WIN11-02\VULN31TEST
```

The test file was read remotely using:

```cmd
type \\WIN11-02\VULN31TEST\test.txt
```

Remote write capability was tested using:

```cmd
echo Remote write validation > \\WIN11-02\VULN31TEST\remote-test.txt
```

The successful test demonstrated that a second endpoint could access and modify data exposed by the overly permissive share.

---

## Security Impact

The finding demonstrated a configuration capable of allowing broad remote access to shared data.

Potential impact in an equivalent production environment could include:

- Unauthorized file access
- Unauthorized file modification
- File replacement
- Data deletion
- Abuse of shared storage
- Increased lateral-movement opportunity

The finding did not involve exploitation of a software vulnerability or CVE.

It was classified as an access-control/configuration vulnerability.

